{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":15000,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"fff33ee72bf247789e1078f1ae90f503b62721bc90454dad52235ce0f04a3afb","packTokens":4784,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"side","tokens":100,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl","tokens":110,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"sketches","tokens":4550,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":7,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"sketches"},"pointers":[],"system":"PRODUCT SIDE. This run builds the API side of the product these requirements describe, in an existing .NET API project: every operation of the locked API contract in contracts/openapi.yaml, exactly as it is written there, and the data behind them. The web app is built in its own repo: no screen is built here, and no criterion is written for one.\n\nThree engineers independently listed behaviours for the same request. Find where they DISAGREE about what the system should do (not wording differences).\nFor each disagreement: id D-1.., the span, a short topic, and the readings: which sketch (1-3), which behaviour index (0-based) in that span, and a one-line summary. Cite at least two different sketches. Empty list if they agree.","tools":[],"user":"<artifact id=\"sketches\" kind=\"sketches\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"sketch\": 1,\n  \"spans\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-1\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: The existing .NET API application serves the product’s API behavior; clients interact with it through HTTP API operations rather than through a screen built in this project.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-2\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat contracts/openapi.yaml as the authoritative, exhaustive definition of API behavior: implement its operations, paths, methods, request and response shapes, status codes, and declared security; use the request’s user stories only as context, not as permission to add undocumented endpoints or alter the contract.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: A client can invoke each operation in the locked OpenAPI contract using its declared method, path, parameters, headers, and request body, and receives the response shape and status codes declared for that operation.\",\n     \"1: The contract’s staff-authorization and receipt-listing/creation operations are available at their declared routes and behave according to their declared inputs, outputs, and access rules.\",\n     \"2: A client can call PATCH /customer/orders/{orderId} (changeCustomerOrder) with the contract-defined request and receive the contract-defined result for changing that order.\",\n     \"3: Requests that fail a contract-defined validation, authorization, or resource-existence condition receive the corresponding contract-defined error response; operations do not silently accept invalid or unauthorized changes.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-3\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Implement persistence for the data required by the contracted operations, rather than treating the broader solution overview as a requirement to implement uncontracted product capabilities.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: Data created or changed through a contracted operation is stored and is available to subsequent contracted operations that list, retrieve, or change that data.\",\n     \"1: Creating a receipt through the contracted receipt-creation operation records it so that the contracted receipt-listing operation can return it; changing an order through the contracted order operation is reflected in later reads of that order.\",\n     \"2: The API preserves the relationships and fields required by the contract when it stores and returns records, so repeated reads do not lose or fabricate contract-defined data.\",\n     \"3: If the configured database connection is missing or the database cannot be initialized, the application does not report a successful startup with usable but non-persistent API operations.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"sketch\": 2,\n  \"spans\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-1\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"API-side product work only; no web, customer-portal, or driver-app screens are built in this project.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: The existing .NET API serves the product’s backend operations over HTTP; this change does not add portal screens or other UI. API consumers can call the backend independently of the web app.\",\n     \"1: API requests that require persisted data read and write the shared backend records rather than returning in-memory or fabricated results. Invalid or unavailable backend configuration does not silently produce successful business-operation responses.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-2\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"The locked OpenAPI document governs operation surface and wire-level details. The supplied current-behaviour summary identifies receipt and authorization operations and the customer-order PATCH operation, but does not expose the full YAML; operation-specific wire fields and statuses therefore remain those defined in that document.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: For every operation in contracts/openapi.yaml, the API accepts the contract-defined method, path, parameters, request fields and content types, and returns the contract-defined response fields, status codes and content types. Required-field, type, enum and format violations are rejected rather than silently accepted or returned as success.\",\n     \"1: The contract-defined authorization operation distinguishes permitted and refused access. Meridian staff access is restricted to Meridian accounts; customer access requires the customer sign-in’s second step. Authenticated users can access only the records permitted by their role, warehouse assignments and customer organisation. Auditors have read-only access; attempts to write are refused. Customer users cannot access another organisation’s orders, deliveries or documents, and customer administrators can manage users only in their own organisation.\",\n     \"2: Contract-defined receipt listing and creation return and persist the receipt data represented by the contract. A receipt request that violates contract validation or the applicable business rules—such as an expired product lot or a write by a user without warehouse authority—is refused and does not create a receipt.\",\n     \"3: Contract-defined customer-order operations, including PATCH /customer/orders/{orderId} (changeCustomerOrder), accept only the contract’s request shape and return its specified result. A customer can change or cancel their own order only while it is before Packed; a foreign organisation’s order, a disallowed status transition, or an invalid change is refused without changing the order.\",\n     \"4: All other operations named in the locked contract are callable at their exact documented routes and enforce their documented request/response and access rules; an undocumented route or unsupported method does not perform a business action.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-3\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat the described workflows as persisted API data and business outcomes where the API contract exposes them; do not infer unprovided endpoint names or wire schemas. Stability, allocation and access rules apply to the shared records, including when requests arrive concurrently or readings arrive late.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: Persist products and their required temperature profiles, including version, effective date, reason and author. A profile cannot be saved without a product profile or with overlapping tiers or gaps; prior versions remain viewable and cannot be changed. Product search works by name or code, and a deactivated product cannot be ordered.\",\n     \"1: Persist each stock receipt separately with product, lot, expiry, quantity, supplier and storage location, even when product and lot number match an earlier receipt. New stock is Quarantined; only a Quality Officer can release it, and stock arriving within 30 days of expiry cannot be released. Past-expiry stock cannot be received. Inbound logger data and the resulting tier-by-tier budget usage are retained for the release decision. Stock moves retain who moved it, when, and the source and destination.\",\n     \"2: Persist customer organisations, their users and addresses, serving warehouse, minimum shelf-life requirement, orders and order lines. An order must have one or more lines, each for at least one pack, a saved address belonging to that organisation, a delivery date permitted by its warehouse calendar and cut-off, and a choice of partial delivery or delivery complete. A duplicate submission of the same order does not create a second order. Invalid dates, addresses, quantities or cross-organisation requests are refused without creating or changing the order.\",\n     \"3: On submission, allocate only Released stock from the serving warehouse, never above available quantity, using earliest expiry first and skipping lots that fail the customer’s minimum remaining shelf life on the requested delivery date; equal-expiry lots with more stability budget used are chosen first. Lines can be split across lots. Complete-only orders reserve no stock unless all lines can be fulfilled; partial orders retain available allocations and back-order the balance. Back orders are filled automatically in submission order when suitable stock is released. Dispatcher overrides require a reason and cannot bypass the Released or shelf-life rules. Concurrent submissions cannot allocate the same stock twice.\",\n     \"4: Persist order status and per-line allocated and outstanding quantities. Customers can change or cancel their own order before packing; cancellation returns allocations to availability. After packing, customer changes and cancellation are refused; a dispatcher may cancel and receives the applicable allocation-restoration result. When allocated stock becomes On Hold, Rejected, Recalled or Expired, remove that allocation, attempt replacement allocation, and make the outcome visible to the dispatcher.\",\n     \"5: Persist warehouse stock quantities and allocation state so available, allocated and quarantined quantities can be viewed by product, lot and warehouse and reconcile to quantity on hand. Stock automatically becomes Expired at the end of its expiry date, is removed from allocations, and the dispatcher is notified.\",\n     \"6: Persist shipper-box type and qualification, box contents by lot and quantity, seal time and operator, and its single assigned logger. Refuse sealing without exactly one logger, assigning one logger to multiple open boxes, packing products with different storage bands, or packing a product outside the box type’s qualified range. Persist routes with date, vehicle, driver, ordered stops and planned arrival times; refuse conflicting driver or vehicle assignments and confirmation when a box exceeds qualified duration less the configured safety margin, identifying the box and excess. Confirmed-route changes are communicated to the driver.\",\n     \"7: Accept and persist temperature readings only from the supplier gateway and for known devices. Duplicate readings are stored once; late and out-of-order readings are placed at their measurement time and trigger recalculation. Future readings and readings before box sealing are retained but marked and excluded from calculations. A device with no reading for 15 minutes is shown as silent; an out-for-delivery silent logger raises an alert. Missing intervals remain visible as gaps, not as assumed in-range time.\",\n     \"8: Persist temperature history for boxes, cold rooms and lots, with readings, applicable bands, excursions and gaps available as chart/table data. An excursion opens on an out-of-band reading and closes after three consecutive in-band readings; tier time is charged to each affected handling unit, combining inbound, cold-room and box history without treating different parts of a lot as having identical histories. Reordered or late readings update excursions, budgets, holds and alerts and retain an audit record of the correction.\",\n     \"9: On an excursion, alert the driver when the box is on a route, the dispatcher and the warehouse Quality Officers within two minutes of receipt of the triggering reading. Alert again when a tier’s remaining allowance falls below 25%, when it is exhausted, or when a not-permitted range is reached. Affected stock is placed On Hold pending the quality decision; a late-discovered excursion affecting delivered stock opens a quality-review case and is shown to the customer.\",\n     \"10: Persist auditable actors and times for consequential changes and decisions, including profile changes, stock movements, allocation overrides and corrected excursion histories. Unauthorized writes, invalid state transitions, impossible allocations, invalid quality or stock actions, and attempts by an Auditor to mutate records are refused without applying the prohibited change.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"sketch\": 3,\n  \"spans\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-1\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"This span means implement the product backend in the existing API project, not build any client application or screen.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: Run the product's server-side API in the existing .NET API project; expose API behavior rather than building portal, customer-portal, or driver-app screens.\",\n     \"1: Keep the API's root health response available; it reports successful health with HTTP 200 when the service is healthy.\",\n     \"2: Keep the API's OpenAPI document and Swagger UI available as API documentation/testing endpoints; these do not substitute for the product operations.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-2\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"The locked OpenAPI document is authoritative for exact operation-level details. The available context identifies only some operations, so their unprovided schemas and statuses are not guessed here.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: For every operation in contracts/openapi.yaml, accept the contract's specified HTTP method, path, parameters, headers and request body, and return the specified response status, headers and body schema. Do not omit contract operations or add different semantics to them.\",\n     \"1: Expose the contract's `/api` operations, including the specified staff-authorization and receipt-listing/receipt-creation operations, rather than treating `GET /` as the business API.\",\n     \"2: Implement `PATCH /customer/orders/{orderId}` (`changeCustomerOrder`) with the contract-defined request and response behavior.\",\n     \"3: For invalid requests, missing resources, unauthenticated callers, or callers lacking permission, return the error statuses and response shapes specified by the contract; do not silently accept invalid input or expose another user's or organisation's data.\",\n     \"4: Do not infer additional API operations, fields, or response behavior from the product narrative when those are not specified by the locked contract.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-3\",\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Implement persisted data and access rules for behaviors that the API contract exposes; the narrative describes domain constraints but does not authorize inventing undocumented API routes or fields.\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     \"0: Persist the records required by contract operations so that creating or changing a record is reflected in subsequent reads, and listing or retrieving records returns the persisted state rather than empty placeholder data.\",\n     \"1: Keep separate receipts traceable even when the same product and lot number are received more than once; retain the receipt's product, lot, expiry, quantity, supplier and storage location when those fields are submitted through the API.\",\n     \"2: Persist order and order-line changes, including requested quantities and delivery details supported by the API; scope customer order and delivery reads to the caller's own customer organisation.\",\n     \"3: Enforce the API's role and organisation permissions on reads and writes: staff access is limited to assigned warehouses except for Quality Managers and Auditors, customer users are limited to their own organisation, and auditors cannot create, change or delete records.\",\n     \"4: Where API operations expose stock receipt decisions, keep received stock quarantined until a permitted Quality Officer release; reject receipt of stock whose expiry date is in the past, and do not permit release when it arrived within 30 days of expiry.\",\n     \"5: Where API operations expose order allocation, allocate only released, physically available stock, select earliest-expiring suitable stock first, respect the customer's minimum remaining shelf life, and never allocate a unit to multiple orders. Reject dispatcher overrides that violate those constraints or lack a reason.\",\n     \"6: Persist temperature readings and related history exposed by the API so duplicate readings are stored once, out-of-order readings are placed at their recorded event time, and future or pre-sealing readings are retained but marked and excluded from calculations.\",\n     \"7: Where API operations expose quality excursions and stock disposition, keep tiered stability-budget usage and holds associated with the affected handling units, and retain changes to corrected histories and decisions in an audit trail.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n</artifact>\n\nList the disagreements."}